      {
        "id": 162272,
        "name": "Chrono",
        "username": "Chrono",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/chrono/{size}/2334_2.png",
        "created_at": "2016-02-16T15:38:28.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>If you have one person doing a lot of the work, so long as they are nice about it, the impact on prep is minimal for the rest of the group. The in-game complication is also a factor. Some games require a lot of math and constant reminders of effects, but just like the card game not everyone has to remember everything.</p><p>I think the RPG could also pull a lot from the mechanics of the card game design to simplify setup. Most games have already-made villains/monsters, and some have pregenerated heroes. Add some pregenerated scenes and environments, and you could have several sessions of mixing and matching everything you need. With a little imagination to keep things interesting, of course.</p><p>It may take some time, but I think a lot of the card game's fans are going to eventually try and then love the RPG!</p>",
